{
  "heading": "Retries: Tac",
  "axis": "language",
  "summary": "The same two annotations in every Tac service language, with the same bounds and the same refusals.",
  "body": "A Tac service can reach its own context, so the policy is the same in both realms (ADR 0031). The annotation, its keys and its limits do not change between realms. Only the spelling in the host language changes. A Rust behavior method takes and returns BehaviorValue, which the page cannot pass, so the Rust service gives the page a plain submit(&mut self, id: String) method and an attempts field, and calls its Retry method send from there. In a Swift service on the web, the wait between two tries and the wait of a blocking MaxAtOnce happen inside the call, so the page waits for that time, as with a sleep in Go or C#. A Java service on the web waits the same way between two tries. A blocking MaxAtOnce in Java that finds no free slot fails with timeout at once, because nothing else on the page can free the slot while the call waits. A Ruby service runs one call at a time, so its MaxAtOnce policy is reject; the build refuses block in Ruby with TY1308. Lua has no tab here, because the build refuses Retry and MaxAtOnce in Lua Tac code with TY1308. A Python service writes each Retry and MaxAtOnce method with `async def`, and the page waits for its answer. C++ has no tab here, because the build refuses Setup and Retry in C++ Tac code with TY1308 (ADR 0099).",
